 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** $O(N \cdot C)$ where $N$ is the number of samples and $C$ is the cost of ray intersection tests (shadow ray + light ray).
-- **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$ auxiliary memory per sample.
-- **Performance:** Depends heavily on the sampling strategy. Importance sampling strategies (Solid Angle, Surface Area) generally reduce variance for large lights compared to Uniform Solid Angle.
-- **Strengths:** Supports multiple sampling strategies for different light sizes and material types. Handles self-intersection artifacts via offset.
-- **Limitations:** Uniform Solid Angle is inefficient for small lights (low hit rate). Importance Surface Area requires careful geometric correction.
-- **When to use:** When rendering scenes with spherical lights and varying material properties (diffuse/specular).
-- **When not to use:** For very large lights where other approximations might be faster, or if the light is not spherical.
